  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 151 PM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nEarly afternoon analysis showed the cutoff low responsible for \nour widespread rain spinning along the southeast NE/KS border \nwith rain and drizzle still falling across most of the area. \nThere are also a few thunderstorms in the NE/KS/IA/MO border \narea which should continue this afternoon, but those should \nremain few and far between in our forecast area. Heaviest precip\nshould exit to our northeast this evening, though guidance \nhints at some patchy drizzle lingering into the overnight hours.\nThat said, model soundings do show a shallow dry layer at the \nsurface with low level cold air advection leading to quite a bit\nof subsidence, so anything that does develop should be pretty \npatchy.\n\nBy Wednesday morning, the low is progged to be near the IA/MN \nborder with some shortwave energy and surface trough rounding \nthe back side and perhaps bringing some additional light rain to\nareas near the NE/SD border. Otherwise, the gusty northwest \nwinds will continue, though a bit weaker, and will continue to \nusher in cooler air. Expect highs in the mid 40s to mid 50s with\nperhaps a little sunshine in southeast NE by late afternoon. \nThe surface trough will continue southeast through the area on \nThursday bringing some additional 20-40% rain chances to much of\nthe area during the day. Precip should exit by Thursday \nafternoon with guidance in good agreement that we'll see very \nlittle additional accumulation. Expect a few hundredths to maybe\na tenth of an inch.\n\nDry weather is favored Friday into the weekend as a shortwave \nridge slides through and larger scale upper level ridging builds\nover the western CONUS, leaving us under northwest flow aloft. \nA few various ensemble members suggest some weak shortwave \nenergy and associated light precip could slide through the \nnorthwest flow, but consensus of those solutions with precip \nkeep any accumulation out of our area. For now, have only about \na 5% chance of any rain this weekend. Otherwise, daytime \ntemperatures will start to creep back up as we see less and less\ncloud cover heading through the weekend. Expect highs in the \nupper 50s and 60s Saturday through Monday. One final note, \nThursday night and Friday night look to be the coolest nights of\nthe week, with portions of northeast NE and west-central IA \nexpected to see temperatures near or below freezing. Obviously, \nit's the time of year where those temperatures are pretty \ncommon, but given the recent warm weather, friendly reminder to \nkeep an eye on the temperature forecast for your location and \ndisconnect outdoor hoses and drain those sprinkler systems as \nnecessary.",
